Empirically, AS0.75 appears to yield reasonably accurate CL scaling in children from adults down to the age of 2–5 years [71–74], and this practice is formally accepted by the European Medicines Agency for this age range [75]. However, below this age range of 2–5 years, AS0.75 often leads to biased CL scaling [76–81]. Proponents of allometric theory argue that this is because theoretical allometry does not take maturation of physiological processes into account. According to them, the theoretical basis of AS0.75 finds itself in scaling between organisms that are fully mature. Children are not small adults; as they grow, organs mature, leading to an increase in organ size and expression and function of metabolic enzymes or transporters in eliminating organs [82–90]. Allometric theory does not take the latter into account; therefore, it is proposed that the theoretical framework must be extended to account for the influence of maturation through the addition of an estimated maturation function. Multiple mathematical functions have been used for this [91], but sigmoidal functions based on post-menstrual age (PMA) are most commonly proposed [1, 57, 91]. In these cases, the maturation function is added to Eq. 5 to yield:6\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
