				\begin{document}$$\text{CL}={\text{CL}}_{\text{STD}}*{\left(\frac{{\text{W}}_{\text{i}}}{{\text{W}}_{\text{STD}}}\right)}^{0.75}* \frac{{\text{PMA}}^{n}}{{\text{PMA}}^{n}+{{\text{MATCL}}_{50}}^{n}}$$\end{document}CL=CLSTD∗WiWSTD0.75∗PMAnPMAn+MATCL50n

Rather than a priori inclusion of preconceived relationships, others have proposed data-driven analyses, in which statistical criteria are used to identify the best size descriptor and most appropriate mathematical function to describe the relationship. For this, the rule of parsimony dictates that the simplest (e.g., linear) function is tested first and that subsequent expansions of the equation (e.g., inclusion of additional parameters, such as exponents) are only retained when statistically significantly improving the fit and when meeting other criteria for model evaluation. When describing data across a large paediatric age range, a bodyweight-dependent exponent (BDE) function, in which the allometric exponent b changes as a function of bodyweight, may prove to be beneficial [69, 104, 105]. Two examples of BDE functions for the exponent b (replacing 0.75 in Eq. 5) as described in Wang et al. [106] and Bartelink et al. [104], are shown in Eqs. 7 and 8, respectively.7\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
