Lean bodyweight is often proposed as size descriptor for individuals with obesity [112], but ideal bodyweight [113], adjusted bodyweight [114], fat-free mass (FFM) [115], and normal fat mass (NFM) have also been proposed [57]. NFM was directly developed to be used in conjunction with AS0.75 [57, 67, 111, 116]. It is based on FFM but estimates a fraction of fat mass called Ffat that “reflects the role of fat as a size driver for changes in structure or function in addition to FFM” [57]. NFM is calculated as:9\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${\text{NFM}} = {\text{FFM}} + {\text{Ffat}}\,({\text{BW}} - {\text{FFM}}).$$\end{document}NFM=FFM+Ffat(BW-FFM).
